00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.440
It handles underexposing and&nbsp;
overexposing very very well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.080
with soft gradations and pleasing colors.
With most of my previous film profiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.480
i like to keep the image a little bit&nbsp;
underexposed, but this profile is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.920
versatile and can be exposed both ways.
My only complaint with this profile is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.640
it shift the yellow towards green very&nbsp;
much when overexposed and s ometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.080
can give wierd hues in the highlights in some&nbsp;
situations. This is caused by the cine4 gamma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.680
generally that's the reason wh y i usually avoided&nbsp;
using cine4, if you have watched my other video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.880
on Film Simulation for sony cameras, you can&nbsp;
notice none of the film profile that i've made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.520
is based on cine4, but this time i needed a&nbsp;
more contrasty cine gamma for this profile.
&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.800
Also cine4 is probably the noisiest from all&nbsp;
cine gammas, it's not a big issue if you enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.400
the grain, but for the cleanest image i wouldstill&nbsp;
use cine1 and it's good to keep that in mind. 
&nbsp;

