&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.840
You can see it looks really&nbsp;
good out of the camera 
&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.520
and it's saving me a lot of time that&nbsp;
i would've spent on color grading
&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.440
the way you can achieve this cold cyan&nbsp;
color is by shooting bellow 2500k
&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.360
If you shoot sony, you know the minimum&nbsp;
kelvin you can choose manually is 2500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.200
bbut if you make a custom white&nbsp;
balance you can actually go even lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.880
o higher than manual limits and&nbsp;
i'll show you how you can do that
&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.560
go into the color temperature menu, you can do&nbsp;
that by going into the photo icon at page 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.120
but i reccommend assigning it to a button, so&nbsp;
it's one step away in any given situation
&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.400
I assigned this menu to the joystick just by&nbsp;
clicking so i can easily access it at any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.240
that is a life saver in many occassions.
scroll down to the custom balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.320
press ok and now find something yellow&nbsp;
and fill the whole screen with it
&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.160
press ok again
now the camera will try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.200
to correct the yellow tint in the image and&nbsp;
will compansate by going under 2500 kelvin
&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.880
save this color as a custom, you can create 3&nbsp;
of these custom colors and trust me they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.560
very helpful, not only for film colors but for&nbsp;
day to day shooting, so use this function
&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.800
I've also saved a creative lut for footage&nbsp;
that is shot at regular kelvin so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.240
